        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Zama</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">202 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the town of Zama in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>; the site is generally, but not universally, believed to be a flat area south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicca-veneria">Sicca</a> (modern El Kef), the Draa el Metnan</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and its Numidian allies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, 29,000 or 30,000 by the usual modern figures, or approximately 40,000 (Bagnall); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, 40,000 by most modern accounts following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, or 50,000 by Appian, with 80 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Carthaginian army was routed when the Roman cavalry returned and charged its rear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> puts Carthaginian dead at 20,000, with as many again captured, and Roman dead at 1,500. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> then accepted peace terms imposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
