    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Figures for both armies are uncertain: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> are usually given as 29,000 or 30,000 (Bagnall says 40,000) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> as 40,000 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>'s authority, against Appian's 50,000.</li><li>The Roman cavalry, sent from the field in pursuit of the Carthaginian horse, returned during the final infantry fight and charged the Carthaginian line in the rear.</li><li>The peace treaty took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s overseas territories and some African ones, set an indemnity of 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> over 50 years and barred it from war without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s permission.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s army had left its horses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for lack of shipping space, and Hannibal also had to wait to train his elephants, so he met Scipio with only about 4,000 cavalry.</p></aside>
    </section>

