    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">219 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> besieges, captures and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, and early the next year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> declares war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> leads an army through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">205 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Scipio is elected consul and given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> as his province, with permission to cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on his own judgement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">204 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Roman army lands near Utica and besieges it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">203 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Scipio's night attacks destroy the Numidian and Carthaginian camps, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> recalls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and Mago from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">202 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Scipio's army defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s near Zama</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">201 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> accepts the peace treaty imposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">146 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroys <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-zama" data-v="97d584bfee" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies draw up facing each other</b> The Romans put their heavy infantry in the centre in three ranks, with Laelius's cavalry on the left and Masinissa's Numidians on the right. Hannibal set elephants before two lines of infantry, a third line well behind them, and cavalry on each flank.</li><li><b>The elephants charge with two lines behind them</b> The Numidian horsemen on each side skirmished with javelins, then Hannibal sent in the elephants and moved his first two infantry lines forward in support. His third line stayed where it stood.</li><li><b>The elephants are turned and the cavalry driven off</b> The velites threw javelins at the elephants and the legions sounded their bugles. Many elephants bolted into the Roman gaps and some back through the Carthaginian cavalry. Masinissa and Laelius charged the disordered horse and chased it away.</li><li><b>The first lines of infantry clash</b> With elephants and cavalry gone from the field, all three Roman ranks and the first two Carthaginian lines advanced. The hastati took heavy losses, but the Carthaginian front line broke and fled round the flanks of the second.</li><li><b>The second line pushes the Romans back</b> The Carthaginian second line fought fiercely and drove the hastati back in disorder. The Romans committed the principes, and the second line then fled, with the hastati pursuing it. The Carthaginian third line did not move.</li><li><b>Both sides re-form into single long lines</b> The Romans recalled the hastati and formed one line, with the principes and triarii at the sides. The Carthaginians rallied survivors of their first two lines to lengthen the veterans' line, so it outflanked the Romans.</li><li><b>The Roman cavalry returns and takes the line in the rear</b> The two lines charged each other and neither gained the advantage. Masinissa's and Laelius's cavalry then came back and charged the Carthaginian line from behind. Its line collapsed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae.jpg" alt="Battle of Cannae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Death_of_Paulus_Aemilius_at_the_Battle_of_Cannae_(Yale_University_Art_Gallery_scan).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Trumbull, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 264 to 241 BCE, lasted 23 years and ended in a Carthaginian defeat. Carthage expanded its territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> from 236 BCE, and in 226 BCE agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> Treaty with Rome, which made the Ebro the northern boundary of the Carthaginian sphere of influence. Rome then made a treaty of association with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, well south of the river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, the de facto ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthaginian-iberia">Carthaginian Iberia</a>, besieged, captured and sacked it in 219 BCE, and early the next year Rome declared war.</p><p>Hannibal invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 218 BCE and inflicted heavy defeats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, where at least 67,500 Romans were killed or captured. Toni Ñaco del Hoyo calls these "great military calamities", and Brian Carey writes that they brought Rome to the brink of collapse. In Iberia, Publius Cornelius Scipio took command of the Roman forces in 210 BCE and drove the Carthaginians out in 206 BCE.</p><p>Scipio was elected consul in early 205 BCE at the age of 31, when the minimum age was 42. Roman opinion was divided on whether an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> was an excessive risk, since Hannibal was still in Italy and the Romans had been driven out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 256 BCE. The compromise gave Scipio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> as his province and permission to cross to Africa on his own judgement, but he could only call for volunteers.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Zama&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Roman preparations, Prelude to battle:</p><ul><li>John Lazenby, Hannibal's War: A Military History of the Second Punic War (Aris &amp; Phillips, 1998)</li><li>Brian Todd Carey, Hannibal's Last Battle: Zama &amp; the Fall of Carthage (Pen &amp; Sword, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army that Scipio took to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> had four legions, each of the Roman pair reinforced to an unprecedented 6,200 infantry, with 300 cavalry each. Modern historians estimate 25,000 to 30,000 men, including perhaps 2,500 cavalry. The core was the survivors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, formed into two legions and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and Scipio trained the army for approximately a year. At Zama the ancient sources agree that 6,000 Numidian infantry and 4,000 cavalry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> supported it. Appian gives 34,500 in all, which modern historians do not accept. They usually give 29,000 or 30,000, though Nigel Bagnall gives 40,000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> normally recruited foreigners, many of them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>. Appian states that its army at Zama numbered 50,000, which many modern historians discount; most give 40,000 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>'s authority, all but 4,000 of them infantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> also had 80 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. His army was made up of the survivors of three armies and he deployed them separately. Light infantry, then the elephants, stood in front of three infantry lines: troops of Mago's expedition, Carthaginians and Africans of poor morale or little training, and about 200 metres behind them Hannibal's veterans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, estimated at 12,000, 15,000 to 20,000 or 20,000.</p><p>Scipio set a principes maniple directly behind each hastati maniple, leaving broad avenues for the velites. Masinissa's cavalry stood on the right and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-laelius">Gaius Laelius</a> led 1,500 Roman and allied cavalry on the left.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Zama&#8221; cites where it covers Roman, Carthaginian, Numbers involved, Initial dispositions:</p><ul><li>John Lazenby, Hannibal's War: A Military History of the Second Punic War (Aris &amp; Phillips, 1998)</li><li>Mir Bahmanyar, Zama 202 BC: Scipio Crushes Hannibal in North Africa (Osprey, 2016)</li><li>Brian Todd Carey, Hannibal's Last Battle: Zama &amp; the Fall of Carthage (Pen &amp; Sword, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first clashes were between the Numidian cavalry on the Carthaginian left and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>'s on the Roman right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> then sent his 80 elephants against the Roman infantry, with his first two lines following. The velites threw javelins at the elephants and fell back, and the Roman infantry sounded bugles. Several elephants on the left fled through the Carthaginian cavalry, and Masinissa charged and routed it. Most of the others ran into the gaps between the Roman maniples, where javelins were thrown into their flanks, and those that came out behind the Roman army were hunted down. Some did charge the hastati and caused heavy casualties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-laelius">Laelius</a> likewise charged the disordered Carthaginian cavalry on the right and swept it from the field.</p><p>The Roman heavy infantry and the first two Carthaginian lines then fought hand to hand. The Roman first line broke the Carthaginian front rank, but the second, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> describes as fighting "fanatically and in an extraordinary manner", pushed the hastati back until the principes joined in. Hannibal's third line did not counter-attack. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> recalled their pursuing hastati and extended their line to match the longer Carthaginian one.</p><p>The two lines charged "with the greatest fire and fury" in Polybius's words, and neither gained the advantage for some time. Then the cavalry of Masinissa and Laelius returned and charged the Carthaginian rear. The line collapsed in a great massacre, and Hannibal was one of the few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> to escape.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Zama&#8221; cites where it covers Fighting in 203 BC, Prelude to battle, Initial charges, Infantry engagement:</p><ul><li>Brian Todd Carey, Hannibal's Last Battle: Zama &amp; the Fall of Carthage (Pen &amp; Sword, 2007)</li><li>John Lazenby, Hannibal's War: A Military History of the Second Punic War (Aris &amp; Phillips, 1998)</li><li>Mir Bahmanyar, Zama 202 BC: Scipio Crushes Hannibal in North Africa (Osprey, 2016)</li><li>Dexter Hoyos, Hannibal's Dynasty: Power and Politics in the Western Mediterranean, 247–183 BC (Routledge, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vermina"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vermina.jpg" alt="Vermina" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vermina">Vermina</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vermina_tetradrachm_both_sides.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> states that 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> were killed and as many again taken prisoner, which accounts for the entire Carthaginian army, and gives Roman losses as 1,500 killed. At least 11 Carthaginian elephants were captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and his surviving companions reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Hadrumetum</a>, where they mustered 6,000 infantry and 500 cavalry. Considering this too few to continue the war, he advised the Carthaginian Senate to make peace on whatever terms it could get.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> looted the Carthaginian camp and Scipio marched back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. During the talks a Numidian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vermina">Vermina</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>, was intercepted and defeated by a mostly cavalry force; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records that more than 16,000 were killed or captured. This was the last battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>.</p><p>The treaty stripped Carthage of its overseas territories and some African ones. It set an indemnity of 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, to be paid over 50 years, took hostages, forbade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, limited the fleet to 10 warships and prohibited war outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and in Africa without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s express permission. Many senior Carthaginians wanted to reject it, but Hannibal spoke strongly in its favour and it was accepted in spring 201 BCE. Scipio was awarded a triumph and the agnomen Africanus.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Zama&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Aftermath, Third Punic War:</p><ul><li>Brian Todd Carey, Hannibal's Last Battle: Zama &amp; the Fall of Carthage (Pen &amp; Sword, 2007)</li><li>Howard H. Scullard, A History of the Roman World, 753 to 146 BC (Routledge, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus.jpg" alt="Scipio Aemilianus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_statue_of_a_Hellenistic_prince,_1st_half_of_2nd_century_BC,_found_on_the_Quirinal_in_Rome,_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme,_Rome_(31479801364).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The main source for almost every aspect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, a Greek sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage in 167 BCE, who wrote The Histories sometime after 146 BCE. His work is considered broadly objective and largely neutral between Carthaginian and Roman points of view, and he interviewed participants from both sides where he could. Modern historians think he treated the relatives of his patron and friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> unduly favourably, but the consensus is to accept his account largely at face value. Much of his account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> survives only in fragments.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> relied heavily on Polybius and is used where Polybius is missing, although Adrian Goldsworthy says his reliability is often suspect and Philip Sabin refers to his military ignorance. Dexter Hoyos describes Livy's account of Zama as bizarrely at odds with Polybius's. Appian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> are described by John Lazenby as clearly far inferior to Livy, and Hoyos accuses Appian of bizarre invention at Zama. Other sources include coins, inscriptions and archaeological evidence.</p><p>Much remains uncertain. The site is generally, but not universally, placed at the Draa el Metnan. Little is known of Scipio's numbers, and the ancient sources do not say what role the 6,000 Numidian infantry took. Historians disagree on whether the elephants were ill-advised, and on whether the Carthaginian second line withdrew deliberately.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The defeat left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> with no army with which to continue the war. The treaty made Carthage clearly subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and Scipio took the agnomen Africanus.</p><p>The prohibition on waging war without Rome's permission had lasting effects. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> repeatedly raided and seized Carthaginian territory with impunity, and Carthage's appeals to Rome were always backed by Rome's Numidian ally. In 149 BCE, fifty years after the Second Punic War ended, Carthage sent an army under Hasdrubal the Boetharch against Masinissa despite the treaty. The campaign ended in disaster at the Battle of Oroscopa, and anti-Carthaginian factions in Rome used the unauthorised action as a pretext to prepare a punitive expedition.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a> began later in 149 BCE when a large Roman army landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and besieged Carthage. In the spring of 146 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> launched their final assault, systematically destroying the city and killing its inhabitants; 50,000 survivors were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The former Carthaginian territories were annexed by Rome and made the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with Utica as its capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Zama in 202 BCE Scipio's Roman and Numidian army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s Carthaginian army, and the Roman cavalry's charge on its rear decided the fight. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> accepted a treaty that left it subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and Rome destroyed the city in 146 BCE.</em></p>

